[Music]  here's a question for you should we make  reading aristotle compulsory in school  you're probably going to say no and i'm  going to say no to with the proviso but  I'm going to say no probably for very  different reasons than you are and if  you're one of the people who actually  says yes that's a really great idea I'm  in sympathy with you but I would also  lay down certain demands that would make  it practically not impossible but pretty  difficult for you to realize that so  here's my rant  [Music]  about a week and a half ago I got into  an interesting discussion actually a  little bit of a debate on Twitter and it  was fairly amicable as far as Twitter  goes you know that can be kind of a  cesspool as far as the discussion the  character limits you don't get me  started on all of that because there's a  lot that can be said about that and it  had to do with this question of making  reading of Aristotle compulsory in  schools now it didn't specify whether  we're talking about kindergarten through  twelfth grade or you know college or any  other kinds of schools just schools and  the person who started it off said  compulsory reading of Aristotle in  school would do a lot more good for the  country than any other social experiment  on offer so it's coming from you know  what we can guess to be a fairly you  know traditionalist right-wing  conservative perspective not necessarily  one that is you know just populist or  anything like that because I think  suggesting Aristotle is already the anti  populist if you know your your Aristotle  and I responded by saying this and this  is what kept the discussion going  compulsory reading of Aristotle that  would turn students off for life  Aristotle requires competent teaching  and application to be useful so I'm  making two points there and then one of  my interlocutors not the original poster  but somebody else chimed in and said  these don't seem to be mutually  exclusive than if you look at the  Twitter conversation you can see that I  messed with them a little bit you know  and said well you know teaching and  application aren't really mutually  exclusively said no no what I mean is  making it compulsory and teaching it  applying it competently and I said yeah  you're right they're not mutually  exclusive  whoever claimed that they were as a  matter of fact I would go so far and  here is my position  this to say that if you want to teach  Aristotle in school let alone make  Aristotle compulsory well then you'd  better find some people who are  competent teachers who really know the  stuff and then you have to go beyond  that it's reading is not enough in order  for you to take a moral philosopher  particularly somebody with the  complexity and depth of and richness of  Aristotle's thought it's got to be  application and even real-life  applications to the lives of the  students or it's not going to click it's  not going to matter and if you can't do  that then don't talk about making it  compulsory because all you're doing then  is setting students up to be turned off  and to never read Aristotle in your in  their life so if your goal is actually  get people to read Aristotle or going  beyond that to you know take what's in  his text it's of such great value and  start changing their lives changing  their communities with it  well by god you'd better have some  competent expositors in the classroom  people who really know the text really  know what they're doing it's not enough  to have fans who you know we're told in  some history of philosophy that  Aristotle did this and that and that's a  good thing you need to actually read  your Aristotle understand it and you'd  better know where you disagree with  Aristotle and why you disagree with him  otherwise you can't be a good teacher of  Aristotle so I got a lot to say about  this and I'm going to go through these  points one by one not necessarily in a  logical order but but I think that this  is stuff that could be quite  thought-provoking for many people so let  me say from the start that I'm actually  probably a bigger fan of Aristotle than  most of the people who would find this  this idea of making reading Aristotle  compulsory think themselves to be in  part because I've spent more than half  of my life actually reading Aristotle  reading him in the original at least as  close to it as we can get  you know the critical versions in Greek  of his tag  I've written on Aristotle I presented on  Aristotle  I've given talk after talk after talk  about Aristotle I've applied Aristotle  within the context of executive coaching  life coaching philosophical counseling  so you can guess I'm somebody who really  really is a fan of Aristotle and I'm not  going to be saying oh no no no don't  have students read that stuff without  some good reason to say that so what is  my good reason well Aristotle is pretty  hard when I first read Aristotle I  couldn't make heads or tails of half of  it and the other half I said well this  is just plain common sense or it's  boring that was you know many many years  ago and I didn't actually have competent  teachers to help me through it and I  started out with Aristotle's categories  and Aristotle's Nicomachean ethics but I  had a sort of adverse reaction to  Aristotle as a smart student so imagine  just throwing these books in students  hands and saying go to it Johnny  go to it Susie read some Aristotle let's  talk about it later well that's going to  have almost the opposite reaction of  what you want what what you need is  people who really understand the text to  sit down with students and you know take  manageable portions manageable chunks  and say we're going to work through this  and then you know if you're going to  keep reading it see how the different  texts are interconnected with each other  which sometimes you can't really do and  once you can read Greek or unless you  you know have somebody else who reads  Greek for you show you where these  connections are so I'm you know I'm  probably one of the biggest fans of  Aristotle and I'm going against the  other Aristotle fans who are you might  say you know fans in the in the sense of  just they they really like the stuff  that he's putting out they have an  emotional attachment to it but they  don't really get into it quite quite so  much I would also add to that other  Aristotle scholars are going  be with me on this you know one of the  people who I particularly like both  personally and in terms of his thought  is Alistair McIntyre he's got a great  piece called plain persons and moral  philosophy that I use when I teach  ethics classes and in it he talks about  that you know the ordinary person is  probably a proto Aristotelian which is  why there can be some subtraction and he  says the worst thing we could possibly  do is just throw the nick of McCann  ethics at students and say here read up  on this it's going to really change your  life because they're going to open it up  and say what the hell is this I don't  know what he's talking about here  everything has ends you know virtuous  activity all sorts of side discussions  about you know can the dead be happy  what the hell is this stuff right  MacIntyre recognizes he says we have to  do a lot of interpretation for these  students and it makes total sense we're  not living in the 4th century BC right  these works were written for different  contexts that doesn't mean that they're  not applicable today but we can't do it  in a cookie cutter sort of way we  actually have to do a lot of adaptation  with to do a lot of application we have  to do a lot of getting students to see  that this connects up with and  illuminates their experience and in  order to do that you have to have done  some of that in your own life with the  Aristotle and not in a cookie cutter  schematic way Aristotle's thought is  incredibly flexible the the way in which  many people apply them is not now here  I'm going to go into another digression  you know when we're talking about making  you know reading and study is something  compulsory within the curriculum I think  that you know if you know your history  of ideas and you know anything about  what scholasticism had turned into and  why the early modern philosophers were  reacting against that and why scholastic  became kind of a byword  and you know way to denigrate somebody  you won't be so eager to to do that with  students you know people like Thomas  Hobbs Rene Descartes they read their  Aristotle they had plenty of people who  said well the answers to this problem  are in this text just read it and it'll  all work out and it didn't satisfy them  why not because it was badly taught it  wasn't really well thought out and and  when it came to the places where  Aristotle actually turned out to be  wrong people tried to figure out ways  they could [ __ ] it to make sure that  you know he didn't turn out to be wrong  and you know why would we want to  replicate something like that today I  just can't see that I would love let me  let me preface this by saying I would  love to see every student in our nation  in the United States actually every  student across the world  reading Aristotle's Nicomachean ethics  reading is politics reading the on the  soul reading all sorts of Aristotelian  works the rhetoric to them particularly  fond of and I'd like to see them read it  not just once to get it over with and  take a test on it but to go back to it  over and over and over again but I know  that if we want that to happen we have  to approach it in a very different way  we can't just impose this on students as  if somehow they're just going to respond  to it and the people who we would have  doing the initial exposition and  interpretation and application I don't  think that most of them would be up to  it I mean think about the current crop  of K through 12 teachers or the current  crop of probably philosophy professors  in universities and colleges I wouldn't  as an Aristotle scholar and as somebody  who teaches Aristotle and somebody who  loves Aristotle I wouldn't trust 99% of  them to competently teach Aristotle and  to do so in a way that wouldn't  managed to turn students off 99% I would  say at this point in time may be among  philosophy professors maybe I'd say only  80% would would get that from me but I  know from interacting with them from  presenting papers at conferences to my  peers how few of them actually remember  very much from their their reading of  Aristotle as students now what would be  the prospect we could of course have  some sort of curriculum in training the  trainers in educating the educators but  who would put that into play when we  when we find some big institution to  back that up the way that say you know  gates backed up the the Common Core I  mean if we could that would be great I'd  be happy to spearhead that that  initiative that would be a worthwhile  thing for me to do with my life if  somebody wanted to throw a lot of money  at it I would happily educate educators  in how to you know lead students into  Aristotle but until that's going to  happen what we'd be stuck with are the  run of the mill for the most part with  the occasional person who really is good  at it and cares about Aristotle and  understands Aristotle so you know the  person that I was interacting with is  totally right compulsory reading of  Aristotle competent teaching of  Aristotle they're not mutually exclusive  at all I would go so far as to say you  can't really have one without having the  other so let's put it as an if/then at a  practical if then if you have competent  teaching exposition interpretation  application then it makes sense to make  reading of Aristotle compulsory but if  you don't have that if you don't have  that condition then it would be a  terrible idea to make reading of  Aristotle compulsory it would be  counterproductive it would turn  more students off from Aristotle it  would turn them away from Aristotelian  modes of thinking which are very  powerful which is again I use in my own  practice and with my clients and I teach  to students and I see them successfully  apply in their own life there's a few  other things I want to say about this as  well what's the mindset that thinks that  just by making reading a particular  author compulsory across the board that  we would produce some useful social  change I think that that has happened a  lot on the left but I think this is a  great example of that happening on the  right as well it's not people on the  Left who are saying oh man we got to  make people read Aristotle instead it's  whatever flavor of the month happens to  be for you no it's going to change  people's consciousness by confronting  them with this book it's mostly people  on the right who are saying yeah we got  it people read Aristotle do these really  work out that way there's just merely  throwing material at people change their  their mindset what is the mindset to  think that that works that's that's what  I'd like that to ask right there and  then you know we can think about the  broader context we should make any sort  of reading compulsory or you know if we  really care about students and we really  care about our society and we think that  something is a valuable text and it's  not something that you know immediately  grasp because almost nothing that's  worthwhile is immediately grasped you  know that's in a way a sign of a  mediocre text the fact that anybody can  get it automatically it's probably wrong  too if we're going to make things  compulsory we really owe it to our  students to find people who understand  that stuff that we're doing and if we  have to train them then we have to train  them if they have to train themselves in  order to be competent expositors great  as part of what education is about you  know if you're an educator your  education should be going on for your  life it's not like you get your  sheepskin and now you're ready to teach  you need to be doing continual education  and you should be more passionate about  the things that you're teaching then  your your beginning level students who  are getting into it for the first time  and getting excited about it there  should be a slow burn going on  throughout your your life and your  teaching I mean you should be better  educated by the end of your life than  you are at the midpoint in your career  that's not the case unfortunately much  of the time so to bring this to a close  should we make reading Aristotle  compulsory in school yes if if we're  going to have an entire cadre and  cohorts of great educators who can  competently explain and interpret  Aristotle and show students how to apply  it to their own lives we're not going to  do that that the answers  [Music]

